WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:02.081
- The global economic
outlook for this year

00:00:02.081 --> 00:00:04.950
might be less gloomy than
previously predicted,

00:00:04.950 --> 00:00:07.590
although that's not the case for the UK.

00:00:07.590 --> 00:00:09.000
Britain is the only member

00:00:09.000 --> 00:00:11.190
of the group of seven
nations whose economy

00:00:11.190 --> 00:00:13.980
is likely to shrink in 2023, according

00:00:13.980 --> 00:00:17.550
to the International Monetary
Fund's January forecasts.

00:00:17.550 --> 00:00:20.790
The country has been grappling
with a cost of living crisis,

00:00:20.790 --> 00:00:23.220
with discontent spilling
out onto the streets

00:00:23.220 --> 00:00:24.510
with months of strikes.

00:00:24.510 --> 00:00:27.750
- We have told the government
there's an increasing problem.

00:00:27.750 --> 00:00:29.730
- While the US and
other nations have begun

00:00:29.730 --> 00:00:32.940
to rebound from the pandemic,
the UK has been struggling

00:00:32.940 --> 00:00:33.993
to turn the tide.

00:00:35.040 --> 00:00:38.130
The UK's flagging economy
now looks set to shrink

00:00:38.130 --> 00:00:40.143
by 0.6% this year.

00:00:41.130 --> 00:00:44.820
Even being outperformed
by sanctions hit Russia.

00:00:44.820 --> 00:00:45.653
Here's why.

00:00:46.530 --> 00:00:48.450
Energy prices worldwide soared

00:00:48.450 --> 00:00:50.940
following Russia's invasion of Ukraine.

00:00:50.940 --> 00:00:54.750
In the UK, inflation is close
to its highest in 40 years,

00:00:54.750 --> 00:00:57.123
largely driven by those high energy costs.

00:00:58.200 --> 00:01:00.420
Britain was hit particularly hard

00:01:00.420 --> 00:01:03.930
as it relies on natural gas for
much of its power generation

00:01:03.930 --> 00:01:05.763
and has limited storage capacity.

00:01:06.630 --> 00:01:10.020
Meanwhile, the US was able to
knock down prices domestically

00:01:10.020 --> 00:01:12.690
after the government released
record amounts of oil

00:01:12.690 --> 00:01:15.540
from its strategic petroleum reserves.

00:01:15.540 --> 00:01:17.940
In many countries, for example, the US,

00:01:17.940 --> 00:01:19.830
inflation is beginning to ease,

00:01:19.830 --> 00:01:23.310
thanks partly to policies
heightening by central banks.

00:01:23.310 --> 00:01:25.470
The Federal Reserve hiked interest rates

00:01:25.470 --> 00:01:29.250
multiple times last year
in a bid to curb inflation.

00:01:29.250 --> 00:01:31.590
It now appears to be paying off.

00:01:31.590 --> 00:01:33.900
Consumer spending is starting to falter,

00:01:33.900 --> 00:01:35.730
and price increases are easing,

00:01:35.730 --> 00:01:38.820
suggesting that the US
economy is slowing down.

00:01:38.820 --> 00:01:41.520
The UK central bank, the Bank of England,

00:01:41.520 --> 00:01:44.910
also raised interest rates
to try to slow inflation.

00:01:44.910 --> 00:01:48.000
Yet UK prices have
continued to rise rapidly.

00:01:48.000 --> 00:01:52.019
Grocery prices surged nearly
17%, and the trade association,

00:01:52.019 --> 00:01:56.163
the British Retail Consortium,
says prices have yet to peak.

00:01:57.150 --> 00:01:59.850
Workers across sectors
have suffered a decline

00:01:59.850 --> 00:02:02.970
in inflation-adjusted wages,
especially those whose salaries

00:02:02.970 --> 00:02:05.070
are paid for by the state.

00:02:05.070 --> 00:02:07.500
Strikes from workers
such as train drivers,

00:02:07.500 --> 00:02:10.530
postal workers, nurses and teachers

00:02:10.530 --> 00:02:13.181
have become a familiar
sight across the UK.

00:02:13.181 --> 00:02:14.197
- (indistinct) The NHS.

00:02:14.197 --> 00:02:15.660
- [Nurses] We are the NHS.

00:02:15.660 --> 00:02:18.060
- Things continue to
increase, except for our pay.

00:02:18.060 --> 00:02:19.860
Our working conditions have not changed.

00:02:19.860 --> 00:02:21.750
In fact, they've got worse.

00:02:21.750 --> 00:02:23.310
- Another problem for growth

00:02:23.310 --> 00:02:26.310
is that the country's employment
rate has yet to recover

00:02:26.310 --> 00:02:27.990
to pre-pandemic levels,

00:02:27.990 --> 00:02:30.510
unlike most other G7 nations.

00:02:30.510 --> 00:02:33.060
The UK is suffering from
a decline in the workforce

00:02:33.060 --> 00:02:36.120
with a number of vacancies
close to record levels.

00:02:36.120 --> 00:02:39.150
Many have taken early
retirement and Britain's exit

00:02:39.150 --> 00:02:41.010
from the EU has put more barriers

00:02:41.010 --> 00:02:43.080
in place for workers from the bloc,

00:02:43.080 --> 00:02:45.573
meaning gaps aren't able
to be filled as easily.

00:02:46.770 --> 00:02:49.830
To compare, in the US
where the unemployment rate

00:02:49.830 --> 00:02:53.040
is at a multi-decade low,
the total workforce rose

00:02:53.040 --> 00:02:55.560
to a new record in January, however,

00:02:55.560 --> 00:02:58.320
there are signs that the
tide could also be turning.

00:02:58.320 --> 00:02:59.153
The Bank of England

00:02:59.153 --> 00:03:01.617
has moderated its
economic growth forecasts

00:03:01.617 --> 00:03:05.010
for the UK, which were
previously very negative.

00:03:05.010 --> 00:03:06.180
It thinks that while Britain

00:03:06.180 --> 00:03:08.550
is still expected to enter a recession,

00:03:08.550 --> 00:03:10.020
that it's likely to be shallower

00:03:10.020 --> 00:03:12.360
and shorter than previously feared.

00:03:12.360 --> 00:03:13.650
And it says that inflation

00:03:13.650 --> 00:03:16.050
is likely to drop sharply this year.

00:03:16.050 --> 00:03:18.120
The Bank of England raised
its key interest rate

00:03:18.120 --> 00:03:19.890
on February 2nd, but it signaled

00:03:19.890 --> 00:03:22.290
that it may pause soon,
meaning the economy

00:03:22.290 --> 00:03:25.110
won't have to endure
further sizable increases

00:03:25.110 --> 00:03:26.313
in borrowing costs.

00:03:27.900 --> 00:03:29.880
- We have seen a turning of the corner

00:03:29.880 --> 00:03:32.530
but it's very early days
and the risks are very large

00:03:33.480 --> 00:03:36.330
and it's really that that I think shapes

00:03:36.330 --> 00:03:37.737
where we go from here.

00:03:37.737 --> 00:03:40.404
(pensive music)

